## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a practical, beginner-friendly end-to-end data pipeline with per-record contracts, canonical schemas, late data handling, and basic data quality reconciliation.

## Key Concepts

- Per-record contracts
- Lightweight schema registry
- Canonical schema and partitioning
- Watermarking for late data
- Data reconciliation and basic validation

## Code Example

```javascript
// Lightweight per-record contract check (pseudo)
function validate(record){
  if(!record) return false;
  const has=(k)=>record[k]!==undefined && record[k]!==null;
  return has('device_id') && has('event_ts') && has('metric');
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate schema drift across regions?
- How would you adjust the test plan for larger spikes in regional feeds?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical data quality, late data handling, and observability in a real-world lakehouse pipeline.

## Key Concepts
- Per-record contracts and simple validator logic
- Upserts into Iceberg star schema with deduplication by event_id
- Late data handling with watermarking (15 minutes)
- Lightweight observability: lineage, conforming vs non-conforming counts, metrics sink
- Trade-offs: Spark Structured Streaming vs Flink for beginner workloads

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo validator
function validate(record) {
  if (!record.event_id || !record.ts || !record.device_id) return false;
  if (typeof record.metrics?.value !== 'number') return false;
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you design the bad_data retention and purge policy?
- Which metrics would you expose to operators for quick health checks?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of batch ETL design with simple data contracts and observable bad data handling.

## Key Concepts

- Per-record contracts and simple validation
- Canonical schema and Parquet lake storage
- Bad data routing with error codes
- Schema drift awareness and basic tests

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-batch ETL skeleton (Node.js) - not actual Spark
function transform(record) {
  const isValid = record.user_id && record.event_ts && ["click","view","purchase"].includes(record.action);
  if (!isValid) return { bad: true, error_code: "SCHEMA_VIOLATION" };
  return {
    user_id: String(record.user_id),
    event_ts: new Date(record.event_ts).toISOString(),
    action: record.action,
    context: record.context || {}
  };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test the ETL with a tiny dataset and verify bad_data counts?
- How would you extend to handle additional fields while preserving the canonical schema?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests privacy-first, scalable analytics across regions with data contracts.

## Key Concepts

- Per-region pseudonymization
- Iceberg upserts
- Event-time windows and lateness handling
- Immutable audits and data contracts
- Spark vs Flink trade-offs for streaming

## Code Example

```javascript
// Region-aware salt hash (Node.js)
const crypto = require('crypto');
function saltedHash(region, id, salts){
  const salt = salts[region] ?? salts.default;
  return crypto.createHash('sha256').update(id + salt).digest('hex');
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test idempotency and audit coverage?
- How would you handle a new region with a different privacy policy?
